{
  "_note": "talos.pipeline.json — recommended for new projects (no PyYAML dependency). Copy to your repo root as talos.pipeline.json and edit for your project. Config file lookup order: $PIPELINE_CONFIG env var, talos.pipeline.yml, talos.pipeline.yaml, talos.pipeline.json, .claude-pipeline.yaml, .claude-pipeline.json, pipeline.yaml, pipeline.json. Defaults come from one table, scripts/pipeline-defaults.sh; run `bash scripts/pipeline-config.sh --show` to see every resolved key and its layer (default, global, repo, env), and see docs/user-guide.md#config-reference for the key reference, the user-level file and secrets (a secret is an env:NAME reference, never a value). Every default stated here is tested against that table. JSON has no comment syntax, so commented-out example keys (see talos.pipeline.yml.example) are described here instead of shown inline: execution.worktree_warn_threshold (default 10) sets the non-active worktree count above which the Step 5 end-of-run sweep warns, e.g. \"execution\": {\"worktree_warn_threshold\": 10}. If using Talos's own bash test suite as your verify command, prefer \"bash tests/run-tests.sh --quiet\" (see talos.pipeline.yml.example) over the plain form -- it prints one line per file plus full output only for failures. limits.max_retries (default 5) sets retries per network call after a rate-limit/transient error (must be a non-negative integer), on top of the original try (up to 6 total attempts by default); Retry-After is honored but capped at 60s, e.g. \"limits\": {\"max_retries\": 5}. verify.qa_mode (ci | local, default ci when merge.required_checks below is non-empty else local) makes QA trust CI instead of re-running the verify list above locally, e.g. \"verify\": {\"qa_mode\": \"ci\"}; verify.targeted (default true) makes the developer run only the tests covering its changed files while iterating, then the full verify list once before its final commit, e.g. \"verify\": {\"targeted\": true}; verify.ci_wait_s (default 900, must be a positive integer) bounds how long QA waits in the foreground for merge.required_checks to go green under qa_mode: ci, via pr-checks-required (scoped to just those checks), e.g. \"verify\": {\"ci_wait_s\": 900}. verify.timeout_ms (default 600000, must be a positive integer) is the explicit timeout in milliseconds substituted into the foreground rule next to every verify and CI-wait instruction in the developer/QA prompts, e.g. \"verify\": {\"timeout_ms\": 600000}. roles.pm_skip_when_spec_present (default true, GitHub only) skips PM when the issue body has an acceptance-criteria heading with checklist items or carries the spec:ready label, e.g. \"roles\": {\"pm_skip_when_spec_present\": true}. roles.docs_mode (auto | always, default auto, GitHub only) gates whether the docs subagent dispatches at all: under auto, Step 3e Phase 1 stamps docs:done directly (no subagent) when the PR's changed paths already cover CHANGELOG.md + README/docs, or are scripts/tests-only with a CHANGELOG entry present; when docs does dispatch under auto it reads only the changed doc paths + CHANGELOG hunk, not the full diff; always restores the pre-#200 behavior (docs always dispatches, always reads the full diff), e.g. \"roles\": {\"docs_mode\": \"always\"}. hooks.pre_dispatch (#181, default \"\" -- disabled) runs a shell command before every stage's prompt is built (all roles, both the native subagent and pipeline-agent.sh adapter paths); its stdout, if non-empty, is prepended to the prompt under a \"## Context\" heading -- non-zero exit, a timeout, or empty stdout is a silent no-op with one stderr line, e.g. \"hooks\": {\"pre_dispatch\": \"my-context-tool\"}. The command receives JSON on stdin: role, issue, pr, repo, base_branch, worktree_path, files_hint[] -- see scripts/pipeline-hooks.sh for the full schema. hooks.post_stage (#182, default \"\" -- disabled) runs a shell command after every verdict, approval, block, and merge -- the outcome-side counterpart to hooks.pre_dispatch, fire-and-forget with the same never-block contract, e.g. \"hooks\": {\"post_stage\": \"my-outcome-sink\"}. The command receives JSON on stdin: event, role, issue, pr, repo, sha, verdict, summary, details, attempt (stage/count/total or null), model, runner, duration_s (or null), ts -- see scripts/pipeline-hooks.sh for the full schema. hooks.timeout_s (default 30, must be a positive integer) bounds how long either hook may run before being killed, e.g. \"hooks\": {\"timeout_s\": 30}. events.enabled (#183, default true) and events.path (default \".talos/events.jsonl\", relative to the MAIN repository root via `git rev-parse --git-common-dir` unless already absolute) control the local audit log every hooks.post_stage payload is also appended to as one JSON line, independently of whether hooks.post_stage itself is configured, e.g. \"events\": {\"enabled\": true, \"path\": \".talos/events.jsonl\"}. Read it with `bash scripts/pipeline-events.sh list|tail|path` -- see scripts/pipeline-events.sh for the full usage. notifications.cmd (#184, default \"\" -- disabled) runs a shell command (via sh -c) for every event that passes notifications.events, after Slack/Discord/Teams/Buzz, with JSON on stdin: event, ref, message, thread_key, fields[] ({label,text,url}), repo, issue -- e.g. \"notifications\": {\"cmd\": \"my-notify-tool\"}. A missing command, non-zero exit, or timeout is a silent no-op with one stderr line -- never blocks the pipeline or the other sinks. notifications.cmd_timeout_s (default 10, must be a positive integer) bounds how long the command may run before being killed, e.g. \"notifications\": {\"cmd_timeout_s\": 10}. markers.verify_authors (default true, #187) makes check-approval-sha/read-attempt trust talos:approval/talos:attempt markers only from an effective trust set: the identity Talos itself is authenticated as (inferred automatically, no config needed) unioned with markers.trusted_authors below; set to false to accept any commenter's marker, silently, as before #187, e.g. \"markers\": {\"verify_authors\": false}. markers.trusted_authors (default unset) lists ADDITIONAL logins to trust, unioned with (not replacing) the inferred identity above -- a bot login is never trusted implicitly, only via this list or by being the inferred identity, e.g. \"markers\": {\"trusted_authors\": [\"github-actions[bot]\"]}. agents.roles.<role>.runner and agents.roles.<role>.runner_cmd (#167, default: falls back to agents.runner / agents.runner_cmd) let a single role use a different backend from the rest of the pipeline, on both the native-subagent and pipeline-agent.sh adapter paths -- e.g. \"agents\": {\"runner\": \"claude\", \"roles\": {\"security\": {\"runner\": \"custom\", \"runner_cmd\": \"my-local-model-cli\"}}} routes only the security role through a local model while everything else stays on claude. Run `bash scripts/pipeline-agent.sh --resolve <role>` to see what a role will actually use. No agents.roles.<role>.runner_args in this release -- runner_args stays global-only. roles.adversarial (default false, #237) turns on an optional pre-merge second opinion that runs after security, attacking the diff for vacuous tests, weak patterns, secret shapes and unverified claims -- e.g. \"roles\": {\"adversarial\": true}. Off by default: zero dispatches, and adversarial:approved is never required by the merge gate. Pair it with agents.roles.adversarial.runner: custom + runner_cmd pointing at a wrapper script around a second, independent backend (e.g. a local llama.cpp llama-server endpoint) -- e.g. \"agents\": {\"roles\": {\"adversarial\": {\"runner\": \"custom\", \"runner_cmd\": \"/path/to/adversarial-runner.sh\"}}}. See docs/user-guide.md's \"Second opinion on a local model\" section for the full worked example. merge.union_paths (default [\"CHANGELOG.md\"], #256) lists glob patterns pipeline-mergebase.sh is allowed to resolve mechanically when they are the ONLY conflicting paths on a CONFLICTING PR -- both sides are kept via `git merge-file --union` (PR side first), no developer dispatch; any other conflicting path still falls back to the developer merge-base task, e.g. \"merge\": {\"union_paths\": [\"CHANGELOG.md\"]}. Per-role model selection (agents.model, agents.roles.<role>.model; native path): the shipped agents/*.md carry no model: line, so this config is the only place a model is set and a role with no model at either level inherits the session model. Values are a full model ID or the aliases opus, sonnet, haiku. Set it once for every repo in the user-level file ${TALOS_HOME:-$HOME/.talos}/talos.pipeline.{yml,yaml,json} (every key is read there except the repo-only ones, which are dropped with a stderr note naming the key); this repo's file is merged over it key by key and wins, e.g. \"agents\": {\"model\": \"sonnet\", \"roles\": {\"security\": {\"model\": \"opus\"}}}. /talos:setup writes the user-level file; run `bash scripts/pipeline-agent.sh --resolve-all` to see each role's model and which layer (project, global, session default) decided it. agents.restamp_model (default: falls back to agents.model, #258) sets the model for re-stamp dispatches -- a cheap delta re-review of a PR a role already approved, used instead of a full stage whenever check-approval-sha --stale-list names a role that already has a (now-stale) prior approval; agents.roles.<role>.restamp_model overrides a single role (precedence: role restamp -> global restamp -> agents.model), e.g. \"agents\": {\"restamp_model\": \"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\"}. agents.effort (default: unset -- the runner's own default, #271) sets the reasoning effort (low | medium | high | max) for every role; agents.roles.<role>.effort overrides a single role (precedence: role effort -> global effort -> runner default), e.g. \"agents\": {\"effort\": \"medium\", \"roles\": {\"validator\": {\"effort\": \"low\"}}}. An invalid value is rejected with a stderr warning and treated as unset. Run `bash scripts/pipeline-agent.sh --resolve <role>` to see what a role will actually use. agents.restamp_effort and agents.roles.<role>.restamp_effort (default: falls back to agents.effort, #271) set the effort for re-stamp dispatches, the same chain shape as agents.restamp_model, e.g. \"agents\": {\"restamp_effort\": \"low\"}. vcs.token_env (default: unset, falls back to GITHUB_TOKEN then GH_TOKEN) names the env var holding the GitHub token for the github-api provider (token-only, no gh CLI); no effect on the github provider, e.g. \"vcs\": {\"token_env\": \"MY_BOT_TOKEN\"}. board.status_map (default: unset) is an optional flat mapping from pipeline status names to your board's actual column names, when they differ from the \"statuses\" display names -- an absent key passes through unchanged, e.g. \"board\": {\"status_map\": {\"Blocked\": \"Needs attention\"}}. merge.forbidden_files_replace (default false) set true restores pre-v0.13 behaviour -- forbidden_files REPLACES the built-in defaults instead of unioning with them; security warning: this suppresses built-in secret-protection patterns for every PR, e.g. \"merge\": {\"forbidden_files_replace\": false}. merge.forbidden_files_allow (default [], empty) lists explicit exemptions checked before deny patterns, e.g. allow a committed template while keeping the real file blocked: \"merge\": {\"forbidden_files_allow\": [\".env.example\"]}. execution.isolation (default \"worktree\") selects the working-copy strategy: worktree (default) -- each developer/QA stage runs in its own git worktree; branch -- stages run in the orchestrator's checkout on a per-issue branch (serialized, max_parallel: 1 only); checkout -- not yet implemented, refused, e.g. \"execution\": {\"isolation\": \"worktree\"}. notifications.buzz_relay (default \"\", overridden by PIPELINE_BUZZ_RELAY) is the Buzz (Nostr) relay URL -- not a secret, identifies a deployment the same way buzz_channel does, e.g. \"notifications\": {\"buzz_relay\": \"wss://relay.example.com\"}. limits.max_total_dispatches (default 8) is the absolute ceiling on total developer dispatches per issue, across all stage changes -- never resets, even when the blocking stage changes, e.g. \"limits\": {\"max_total_dispatches\": 8}. Evidence capture (#405, part of #352) is off by default and has no JSON block here; the keys are evidence.enabled (strict true/false, default false), evidence.command (string, at most 2000 characters, no NUL), evidence.dir (relative path, at most 200 characters from [A-Za-z0-9._/-], no leading - or /, no .. component, not \".\", no .git component), evidence.include (1-20 basename globs of 1-64 characters matching [A-Za-z0-9*?._-], no /), evidence.when (user-facing | always, default user-facing), evidence.store (attach, default attach: uploaded with gh pr comment --attach), evidence.max_files (integer 1-100, default 10) and evidence.max_mb (integer 1-100, default 20). An invalid value warns once and reads as absent, e.g. \"evidence\": {\"enabled\": false}. Runner failover (#418, default: unset, no chain) is described here, not shown inline: agents.fallback is an ordered list of 1-5 runner names (claude | pi | codex | gemini | antigravity | custom, no duplicates) that pipeline-agent.sh tries in turn when a runner dies with a provider error (exit 75 on any runner, or a recognised claude rate-limit, quota, overload, auth or network line); agents.roles.<role>.fallback overrides it for one role; agents.provider_down_s (integer 60-86400, default 900) is how long a failed provider stays marked down in .talos/providers.json. A task failure never fails over, and neither does a stage that already wrote (exit 69). Entries are runner names only: the fallback runner uses its own default model, agents.runner_args is not forwarded, and custom uses runner_cmd. e.g. \"agents\": {\"fallback\": [\"codex\", \"gemini\"], \"provider_down_s\": 900}. An invalid value warns once and reads as absent. agents.capture_usage (#420, default true) records each pipeline-agent.sh attempt's token usage in its stage event: claude is run with --output-format json (after agents.runner_args, before the prompt) and only the message text is printed; set it to false, or put --output-format in agents.runner_args, to leave the invocation alone and keep tokens null. Only a literal false turns it off. A custom runner_cmd reports usage by writing a JSON object {\"tokens\": N, \"tool_uses\": N, \"model\": \"...\"} (all optional) to the file named by TALOS_USAGE_FILE, e.g. \"agents\": {\"capture_usage\": true}.",

  "base_branch": "dev",

  "vcs": {
    "provider": "github",
    "token_env": "MY_BOT_TOKEN"
  },

  "release_branch": "main",

  "board": {
    "enabled": true,
    "project_number": 1,
    "owner": "myorg",
    "status_field": "Status",
    "statuses": {
      "ready": "Ready",
      "in_progress": "In progress",
      "in_review": "In review",
      "done": "Done",
      "blocked": "Blocked"
    },
    "status_map": {
      "Blocked": "Needs attention"
    }
  },

  "verify": [
    "python -m pytest tests/ -x -q"
  ],

  "merge": {
    "auto": true,
    "method": "squash",
    "required_checks": [
      "test"
    ],
    "delete_branch": true,
    "forbidden_files_replace": false,
    "forbidden_files_allow": [
      ".env.example"
    ],
    "approval_waiver_paths": [
      "*.md",
      "docs/**",
      "CHANGELOG.md"
    ],
    "union_paths": [
      "CHANGELOG.md"
    ],
    "auto_sync": true
  },

  "issues": {
    "label_filter": "pipeline:ready",
    "skip_labels": [
      "pipeline:blocked",
      "wontfix"
    ],
    "max_parallel": 1
  },

  "execution": {
    "isolation": "worktree"
  },

  "roles": {
    "validator": true,
    "pm": true,
    "qa": true,
    "reviewer": true,
    "security": true,
    "adversarial": false,
    "docs": true,
    "planner": false,
    "changelog_fragments": false
  },

  "comments": {
    "enabled": true,
    "header": "**Agent:** {role} (talos)",
    "templates_dir": "templates/comments"
  },

  "notifications": {
    "slack_channel": "",
    "discord_channel": "",
    "buzz_channel": "",
    "buzz_relay": "",
    "templates_dir": "templates/notifications",
    "threading": true
  },

  "status": {
    "enabled": false,
    "file": "TALOS_STATUS.md",
    "log_heading": "## Log",
    "resume_heading": "## Resume here",
    "fragments_dir": "docs/status.d",
    "archive_dir": "status/archive",
    "log_days": 30,
    "log_max": 50,
    "resume_max_lines": 40
  },

  "limits": {
    "max_fix_attempts": 3,
    "max_total_dispatches": 8,
    "max_retries": 5
  },

  "pr": {
    "draft": true
  }
}
